1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,220
A thousand dollars a day won't finish a billion for twenty-seven centuries.

2
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:07,920
Sounds fake? Here's the math, nice and slow.

3
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:12,860
Say someone hands you a million dollars, and you must spend a thousand every day.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,600
It's gone in about two years and nine months.

5
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:17,860
Tap the heart if you'd make it to year two.

6
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:21,640
Now they hand you a billion. Same rule, a thousand a day.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
How long does that take? Two thousand seven hundred thirty-eight years.

8
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,600
You'd have to start around seven hundred BC to be broke today.

9
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:32,340
That's when ancient Rome was basically a small town.

10
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:37,720
Because a billion isn't ten millions, or a hundred. It's a thousand of them, stacked.

11
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:41,280
Send this to the friend who says a billion is just a lot of millions.

12
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:45,300
Want it even simpler? Count one number per second, no breaks.

13
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:48,220
A million seconds is about eleven and a half days.

14
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,420
Follow if this just changed how you hear the word billion.

15
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:54,640
A billion seconds? Almost thirty-two years.

16
00:00:54,640 --> 00:00:57,620
That's the gap between rich and stupid rich.

17
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:01,760
So next time someone says a billion like it's nothing, remember ancient Rome.

18
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:04,400
How long would your million last? Drop a number.

